      "location": "title",
      "defect": "overstrong-title-or-statement",
      "evidence": "The dependency [[def-small-set-minorization-and-drift]] defines smallness by P^m(x,A) ≥ ε ν(A), without requiring ν(C)=1. For X={0,1}, let P swap the states, C={0}, V(0)=1, V(1)=2, λ=1/2, b=2, δ=1, ν=δ_1, and π=(1/2,1/2). The one-step minorization, drift inequality, and invariant law hold, but P^n(0,·) alternates between point masses, so its TV distance from π is 1/2 for every n. The Statement excludes this example by requiring νγ(C)=1; the title omits that essential strong-aperiodicity condition and overstates what common drift and minorization alone give.",
